                    briefing_title_r1:      `Round 1 — Diagnose the 60 bps gap`,
                    briefing_text_r1:       `Three years of returns, a holdings file with ESG ratings and carbon data, the fee schedule and a flows history are on your desk. The fund trails MSCI Europe by <strong>60 bps net</strong>, yet gross alpha is only <strong>≈ +25 bps</strong> — the <em>0.85% fee</em> turns the edge negative. Decompose the gap into selection, allocation, fee and screening effects. The quant desk wants to tilt into last year’s winners; your own back-test says <em>persistence reverses after costs</em>. Name the dominant driver, judge whether past wins were skill or luck, and separate what is structural from what is fixable.`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Choose the SRI screen (and match the claim)`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `Set the responsible-investment policy along the spectrum — light exclusions, a rating floor, best-in-class tilting, or full <strong>Article 9 fossil-free</strong> (which excludes ~22% of the benchmark and drives tracking error toward 4.0–4.8%). ter Horst’s finding: responsible screening <em>need not</em> destroy risk-adjusted return, but it reshapes the risk profile. Then decide the <strong>SFDR claim</strong> — a compliance decision, not a sales one: your factsheet must match your holdings, or the AFM opens a greenwashing inquiry that hits flows across <em>every</em> fund. An excluded energy stock just jumped +18%; hold your discipline or chase it?`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Construct: persistence, risk budget, fee & flows`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `Build the portfolio inside your screen. Decide how much weight to give <strong>performance-persistence signals</strong> (tilting toward trailing winners) versus valuation and quality — remembering your back-test says persistence <em>reverses after costs</em>. Set your <strong>active-risk budget</strong>; an aggressive bet on top of an Article 9 screen risks accidental concentration. Address the <strong>fee</strong>: no screen fixes a 0.85% fee that exceeds your 25 bps gross edge when the client can buy index-plus-ESG at 0.20%. And prepare a <em>flows-contingency plan</em> — strong performance pulls in €250M; lagging triggers a €200M redemption that forces selling.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Recover & defend before the client and board`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `Day 90. The realized scorecard is in — net alpha, tracking error, ESG integrity and net flows, shaped by your screen and construction. Present to the pension CIO and the Meridiaan board a <strong>defensible</strong> answer to the founding question: did the responsible, active strategy add value net of fees, or should it convert to passive? The CIO presses: <em>“Show me the SRI screen cost or earned its keep — and prove your alpha was skill, not luck.”</em> Answer with evidence and the attribution, not ideology — and own the trade-off you made.`,

