                    coach_default:          `Allocation logged.`,
                    coach_r1_fee:           `<strong>That is the right first cut.</strong> Gross alpha is ≈ +25 bps and the fee is 0.85% — the fee alone is ~60 bps of the drag, and it is the most structural part of the gap. No screen and no stock-picking heroics fix a fee that exceeds your edge. Naming the fee is naming the problem.`,
                    coach_r1_selection:     `<strong>Blaming stock selection misses the structural part.</strong> Even perfect selection only earns ≈ +25 bps gross — the 0.85% fee still turns it negative net. You can chase better names forever and the fee will keep eating the alpha. Decompose the gap before you prescribe a cure.`,
                    coach_r1_luck:          `<strong>“Wait for mean reversion” is not a diagnosis.</strong> A 60 bps net gap that is mostly fee is structural, not noise — waiting changes nothing while the client’s 90-day clock runs. Separate what is luck from what is the fee before you do nothing.`,
                    coach_r1_screen:        `<strong>Loosening the screen to “catch up” is exactly backwards.</strong> ter Horst’s evidence is that responsible screening need not destroy risk-adjusted return — the dominant drag here is the fee, not the light screen. Weakening ESG to chase return also walks you toward a greenwashing problem.`,
                    coach_r1_persiststrong: `<strong>Tilting hard into last year’s winners is the persistence trap.</strong> Your own back-test shows top-quartile performance persists weakly and reverses after costs. Betting against your own evidence adds active risk and transaction costs without net return — Round 3 will make you pay for it.`,
                    coach_r1_persistignore: `<strong>Ignoring the rankings entirely overcorrects.</strong> Persistence is weak, not zero — it is concentrated at the extremes and reverses after costs. Use it as one signal among valuation and quality, weighted lightly, rather than throwing the data away.`,
                    coach_r1_skillskill:    `<strong>“Bank on the manager repeating it” skips the test.</strong> Gross alpha of 25 bps over three years is well inside the range you would see from luck alone. Until you separate alpha from beta and check persistence, you cannot tell the board this was skill — and they will ask.`,
                    coach_r1_skillluck:     `<strong>Declaring it pure luck and converting now is premature.</strong> You have not yet run the screen, the construction, or the fee decision — there may be a defensible, evidence-based active strategy here. Test the edge before you give up the franchise on day one.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You named the fee as the structural driver, read persistence as weak-and-reversing, and refused to call past wins skill until proven. That is the evidence-based footing ter Horst demands — now build a portfolio that earns its keep net of that fee.`,
                    coach_r2_light:         `<strong>Light exclusions keep the status quo — and the structural gap.</strong> Article 8 barely shrinks the universe, so tracking error stays low, but the pension client wants a credible green strategy. A light screen is safe on risk and weak on the mandate; it does not, by itself, answer the client’s ultimatum.`,
                    coach_r2_bestinclass:   `<strong>Best-in-class is the disciplined green path.</strong> You keep most sectors and overweight ESG leaders within each, so the universe barely shrinks and tracking error stays controlled — exactly ter Horst’s point that responsible screening need not sacrifice risk-adjusted return. Now make sure your SFDR claim matches it.`,
                    coach_r2_article9:      `<strong>Full Article 9 is defensible — but it is a risk decision, not only an ethics one.</strong> Excluding ~22% of the benchmark drives tracking error toward 4.0–4.8% and concentrates the book. Going green is fine; going undiversified by accident is not. Whatever you do in Round 3, do not stack an aggressive risk budget on top of this.`,
                    coach_r2_greenwash:     `<strong>That is greenwashing.</strong> Your holdings still look like an Article 8 book but your factsheet says “Article 9, fossil-free.” The AFM has opened an inquiry and Morningstar flagged it — and the outflows are hitting every fund in the firm, not just this one. The claim has to match the holdings. Full stop.`,
                    coach_r2_overclaim:     `<strong>You stretched the claim past the holdings.</strong> A best-in-class book is genuinely responsible, but marketing it as “Article 9, fossil-free” overstates it — and a single bad disclosure is all the AFM needs. The reputational hit spreads across the firm. Claim what you actually hold.`,
                    coach_r2_under:         `<strong>Under-claiming wastes a real green book.</strong> You built a credibly responsible portfolio and then hid it behind an Article 8 label — you carry the tracking-error cost of the screen without getting the mandate credit for it. Match the disclosure to the holdings, no more and no less.`,
                    coach_r2_soften:        `<strong>One excluded stock pops +18% and you soften the screen.</strong> That is the persistence temptation in ESG clothing — you set a policy precisely so a single name’s move would not blow it up. Softening now muddies your claim and your ESG integrity. Discipline is the whole point of a screen.`,
                    coach_r2_panic:         `<strong>Tearing up the screen to chase the rally is the worst of all worlds.</strong> You abandon the policy on one earnings beat, wreck your ESG integrity, and — if your factsheet still says green — hand the AFM a greenwashing case. The rally is not the test; your discipline is.`,
                    coach_r3_persist:       `<strong>You bet heavily on trailing winners — against your own back-test.</strong> Two quarters on, those winners mean-reverted, tracking error spiked, and after transaction costs the net alpha is worse, not better. ter Horst’s persistence finding just cost you real bps. Why did you bet against your own evidence?`,
                    coach_r3_concentration: `<strong>Aggressive risk on top of an Article 9 screen is the concentration trap.</strong> You excluded ~22% of the benchmark and then ran big active bets in a 28-name book. One holding missed earnings and you drew down hard relative to the index. Where was your active-risk budget? Green is defensible; undiversified by accident is not.`,
                    coach_r3_risk:          `<strong>An aggressive risk budget without an edge just adds noise.</strong> Big bets only pay if you have conviction that earns the active risk. Spent indiscriminately, a ~5% tracking error widens your outcomes without improving your expected net alpha — and the client reads volatility as a lack of control.`,
                    coach_r3_fee:           `<strong>You perfected the screen and left the fee at 0.85%.</strong> No screen fixes a fee that exceeds your 25 bps gross edge — and the client can buy index-plus-ESG at 0.20%. What is your fee actually buying them? Net-of-fee thinking dominates; the fee is a strategic variable, not an administrative one.`,
                    coach_r3_feeraise:      `<strong>Raising the fee for the “green premium” is the wrong direction.</strong> Your net alpha is already negative and the client has a 0.20% alternative — charging more widens the very gap you were asked to close. A premium label does not justify a premium fee when the net number is red.`,
                    coach_r3_firesale:      `<strong>Selling your most liquid winners to meet the redemption guts the book.</strong> You raised cash fast and left a worse, more concentrated portfolio behind — crystallizing costs and shrinking the fee base. That is the flows-cost feedback loop in action. A liquidity sleeve would have absorbed the shock without selling your best names.`,
                    coach_r3_flows:         `<strong>You managed the portfolio as if money sat still.</strong> Performance lagged, the redemption hit, and with no plan you were forced into value-destroying sales — a worse book and a smaller fee base. Flows change what you can hold; ignoring them is how a paper portfolio meets reality.`,
                    coach_r3_strong:        `<strong>That is construction with discipline.</strong> Light weight on persistence, a deliberate active-risk budget, a fee brought below your gross edge, and a liquidity sleeve to ride the flows. Net alpha can finally turn positive without betting the book — exactly the evidence-based path the founding question demands.`,
                    coach_r4_evidence:      `<strong>That is the answer the CIO asked for.</strong> You quantified what the screen cost or earned in THIS portfolio and tested whether the alpha was skill — not an ideological claim either way. Boards trust a number with its confidence interval far more than a belief. ter Horst’s question, answered empirically.`,
                    coach_r4_ideologypro:   `<strong>“It is the right thing to do” is not an analysis.</strong> The CIO did not ask whether you like SRI; they asked whether, in this portfolio this period, the screen cost or earned alpha and whether the alpha was skill or luck. Show the number, not the belief — ideology, even pro-ESG, loses the mandate.`,
                    coach_r4_ideologycon:   `<strong>“A return drag we tolerate” is the same mistake from the other side.</strong> You have pre-judged the screen as a cost without measuring it — and ter Horst’s evidence is that responsible screening need not destroy risk-adjusted return. Measure it in your book before you concede it as a drag.`,
                    coach_r4_passive:       `<strong>Converting to passive when your edge survived is conceding too much.</strong> If your net alpha is defensible and your ESG claim is clean, you are giving up a franchise that earned its keep. Convert when the evidence says the edge is gone — not before.`,
                    coach_r4_closet:        `<strong>A closet-index fund at active prices is the worst outcome.</strong> You keep the 0.85% fee but hug the benchmark — paying for active and delivering passive. The client will spot it, the regulator dislikes it, and the mandate leaves. Either earn the fee actively or cut it; do not pretend.`,
                    coach_r4_attribution:   `<strong>The attribution is exactly the right defense.</strong> Splitting the result into selection, allocation, fee and screen — with the persistence test attached — lets you prove what was skill and own what was a deliberate trade-off. That is governance, not salesmanship, and it is what keeps the mandate.`,
                    coach_r4_cherrypick:    `<strong>Pointing at the one good quarter is how boards stop trusting you.</strong> They can see the full record. Highlight your best print and downplay the rest and they will assume the rest is worse than it is — and ask why you could not show the attribution.`,
                    coach_r4_blame:         `<strong>Blaming the benchmark and the regulator abdicates the seat.</strong> The MSCI Europe comparison and the SFDR rules are the rules of the game you took on. An evidence-based manager owns the result and explains the trade-off; blaming the scoreboard tells the board you were not in control.`,

                    sum_default:            `Decision made.`,
                    sum_r1_good:            `Named the fee, read persistence as weak, refused to assume skill.`,
                    sum_r1_weak:            `Mis-read the driver, persistence, or skill-vs-luck.`,
                    sum_r2_good:            `Disciplined screen with a matching SFDR claim; held the line on the event.`,
                    sum_r2_greenwash:       `Claimed greener than the holdings — greenwashing exposure.`,
                    sum_r2_article9:        `Full Article 9, claim matched — green but concentrated.`,
                    sum_r2_weak:            `Screen or claim under-tuned.`,
                    sum_r3_good:            `Light persistence, deliberate risk, fee cut, liquidity sleeve.`,
                    sum_r3_persist:         `Chased trailing winners — the persistence trap.`,
                    sum_r3_concentration:   `Aggressive risk on an Article 9 screen — concentration drawdown.`,
                    sum_r3_fee:             `Left the fee above the gross edge.`,
                    sum_r3_weak:            `Construction partially disciplined.`,
                    sum_r4_good:            `Evidence-based verdict, defensible franchise, owned the attribution.`,
                    sum_r4_weak:            `Answered with ideology, cherry-picking, or a closet index.`,

                    insight_smart:          `Smart AND ethical: you ran the evidence-based path — a disciplined, credibly green screen with a matching SFDR claim, light weight on reversing persistence, a fee brought below your gross edge, and a flows plan that protected the book. Net alpha turned positive without runaway tracking error, the ESG claim held, and you can prove to the board the screen earned its keep. That is ter Horst’s question answered empirically.`,
                    insight_greenwash:      `Greenwashing finding: your factsheet claimed greener than your holdings supported, the AFM opened an inquiry, and the reputational hit triggered outflows across every fund in the firm — not just this one. The screen and the construction barely mattered once the claim broke from the holdings. Disclosure must match the book.`,
                    insight_persistence:    `Persistence trap: you tilted hard into last year’s winners against your own back-test, those winners reversed, tracking error spiked, and after transaction costs the net alpha was worse than the 60 bps you started with. ter Horst’s finding held — persistence is weak, concentrated at the extremes, and overwhelmed by costs.`,
                    insight_concentration:  `Concentration drawdown: you stacked an aggressive active-risk budget on top of a full Article 9 screen, leaving a concentrated book that drew down hard when a single holding missed. Going green was defensible; going undiversified by accident was the avoidable error. The screen was a risk decision you under-managed.`,
                    insight_fee:            `Fee drag: you perfected the screen but left the 0.85% fee above your ≈ +25 bps gross edge, so net alpha stayed negative while the client had a 0.20% alternative. No screen fixes a fee that exceeds the edge — the fee was the first-order strategic variable, and it went unaddressed.`,
                    insight_ideology:       `Ideology over evidence: you answered the founding question with belief — pro- or anti-SRI — rather than measuring whether, in this portfolio this period, the screen cost or earned alpha and whether the alpha was skill or luck. The board governs on numbers; an ideological answer, in either direction, lost their confidence.`,
                    insight_mixed:          `A mixed result: some decisions were disciplined but the screen, the fee, the risk budget and the flows plan did not fully reinforce each other. Enough was left on the table that the board cannot yet conclude the responsible, active strategy clearly earned its keep net of fees.`,
                    insight_flows:          `You managed the book as if money sat still. When the redemption hit, you were forced into value-destroying sales that crystallized costs, shrank the fee base, and left a worse portfolio — the flows-cost feedback loop the simulation makes tangible.`,
                    insight_fee_drag:       `The 0.85% fee quietly exceeded your gross edge for too long. Even good screening and construction cannot rescue net alpha when the fee is the largest single drag and a cheaper, comparable product is one phone call away.`,
